{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310638,"nonce":"kCrkFOQQPkX3Zhd5rCSlV/uE71c=","title":"Runtime crashes, regressions, and WASM-specific issues","message":"**Symptom:** A class field declared as `var x:T = something` is left null\nat runtime even after `new Foo()` — `something` never executes.\nInitialising in the constructor body worked (`function new() { this.x = something; }`).\n\n**Repro:** `class Box { var num:Int = 42; } new Box().num` returned 0.\n\n**Root cause:** Two-stage gap. TAST→HIR `lower_constructor` wasn't\nthreading `TypedField.initializer` into the synthesised\n`HirConstructor.field_inits`. Then HIR→MIR `lower_constructor_body`\n(the active path — `lower_constructor` was dead code) also wasn't\nconsuming `field_inits`.\n\n**Fix:** `tast_to_hir.rs` — `lower_constructor` now accepts\n`class_fields` and populates `field_inits` from each non-static field\nwith an `initializer`. `hir_to_mir.rs` — `lower_constructor_body`\nemits a Store for each `field_init` after super() but before the\nuser-written body so explicit body assignments win. Dead\n`lower_constructor` removed.\n\n\n**Symptom:** A `static var` modified by one function call appears\nunmodified in a subsequent call. `register()` pushed two entries onto a\nstatic `Array`; the immediate next call to a sibling method saw the\narray as empty.\n\n**Repro:** any class with `static var entries:Array\u003cT\u003e = []` and methods\nthat `entries.push(...)` then read `entries.length` from another call.\nEach call observes a fresh empty array.\n\n**Workaround:** instance-based state. Construct a `new Foo()` and pass\nthe reference around. Tested at `nue.arch.ArchRegistry` — same code\nworks correctly as instance state, fails as static.\n\n**Priority:** MEDIUM — static singletons are a common pattern; right now\nevery shared registry has to be threaded explicitly through callers.\n\n\n**Now working:**\n- `var c = new Impl(); c.name()` — concrete receiver.\n- `var i:I = new Impl(); i.name()` — Let-site wrap (already worked).\n- `function f(b:I) { b.name(); }` called with `f(new Impl())`.\n- `register(b:I) { entries.push(b); }` then read back + dispatch.\n- `Reg.entries.push(new Impl())` direct on `Array\u003cI\u003e` static field.\n- `for (e in arr) e.name()` iterating `Array\u003cI\u003e` with multiple impls.\n- Return-by-interface from helper functions.\n\n**Edge case also fixed (2026-05-20 follow-up):**\n- Array literal with class elements assigned to `var:Array\u003cI\u003e`:\n  `var arr:Array\u003cI\u003e = [new Impl(), new Impl2()]; arr[0].name()` works.\n  The MIR Let handler now detects when the init is `HirExprKind::Array`\n  AND `type_hint` is `Array\u003cInterface\u003e`, and routes through\n  `lower_array_literal(elements, type_hint)` instead of using\n  `expr.ty` (which the typechecker inferred from the first element).\n  `lower_array_literal` then wraps each Class element in a fat pointer\n  using the declared interface element type.\n\n**Fixes applied:**\n1. `hir_to_mir.rs::maybe_materialize_for_call` gained a Path 3 that\n   wraps Class→Interface at user-function call boundaries (the existing\n   anon-materialization path was the model). The wrap is tracked in\n   `interface_wrapped_args` so the three temp-heap-values arg-tracking\n   sites skip emitting Free after the call (the wrapped fat ptr may\n   escape via the callee storing it in long-lived structures).\n2. `tast_to_hir.rs::maybe_promote_class_arg_to_interface` inspects the\n   receiver's container element type at MethodCall lowering and wraps\n   each Class arg in a `HirExprKind::Cast` when the element type is an\n   Interface. This routes through MIR's existing Class→Interface Cast\n   arm and produces the fat pointer wrap. Handles\n   `Array.push`/`unshift`/`set` and similar generic methods.\n3. `hir_to_mir.rs::lower_expression` post-wraps any expression whose\n   `expr.ty` was promoted to an Interface and whose underlying kind\n   reveals a class (covers return values + a few minor cases).\n\n**Suspected location of the literal fix (deferred):** the array literal\nneeds the assignment target's element type plumbed in. Either patch\n`tast_to_hir.rs` to insert Cast nodes for each literal element when the\nliteral is used in a slot whose declared element type is an interface,\nor pass `var_type` into `lower_array_literal` from the Let handler.\n\n\n**Was:** Adding `Dynamic`-typed fields to `nue.transformer.RMSNorm` and\nits peers caused SIGILL inside the broader `transformer-pieces/Main.hx`\nexample (GQAttention + KVCache + TransformerBlock all in play).\n\n**Root cause:** as suspected, this was a downstream symptom of the same\nfamily as Quirks 1 + 3 — field initializers not threading through the\nconstructor (so `this.gpu = null` never ran, leaving `gpu` at whatever\ngarbage was on the heap) combined with interface dispatch reading\nundefined memory in the larger module composition.\n\n**Verified fix:** with Quirks 1 + 3 patched, augmenting `RMSNorm` with\n`public var gpu:Dynamic; public var weightGpu:Dynamic;` + explicit\n`null` initialisation in the constructor runs `transformer-pieces`\nend-to-end on top of all three modules. Module-level GPU dispatch\nwrappers are now safe to wire (deferred until consumers actually need\nthem — `rayzor.gpu.GPUCompute.rmsNorm`/`.rope` still work as direct\nentry points).\n\n\n**Symptom:** A user class with a constructor like\n```haxe\npublic function new(weight:Tensor, eps:Float, paramName:String = \"norm.weight\") { ... }\n```\ncalled as `new RMSNorm(weight, 0.00001)` (omitting `paramName`) leaves\n`paramName` as the empty string `\"\"`, not the declared default `\"norm.weight\"`.\nPassing the value explicitly works.\n\n**Where surfaced:** `nue/transformer/RMSNorm.hx` + `nue/Linear.hx` constructors.\nString default args on regular (non-extern) Haxe classes don't get filled in\nat the call site.\n\n**Workaround:** require callers to pass the name explicitly. For nue's\n`Module.parameters()` consumers (the GGUF loader, eventually), explicit\nnaming is the right contract anyway.\n\n**Suspected location:** the default-arg-fill mechanism in `hir_to_mir.rs`\n(`fill_default_args`, ~line 9990) — works for Int/Float (verified on Phase\n1c NumaPool tests) but appears to drop String defaults. Numeric defaults\nin stdlib MIR wrappers (`takeOwnership:Int = 0`) also work. Likely the\ngap is \"user-defined class constructor + String literal default.\"\n\n**Priority:** LOW — easy workaround; will resurface when GGUF loader (Phase 7)\nexpects model-config defaults.\n\n**Symptom:** `cargo run --release -p compiler --example test_rayzor_stdlib_e2e` hangs at L6 execution of `thread_spawn_basic`. Codegen passes but thread join blocks.\n**Status:** Param type mapping was reverted to safe defaults (all Ptr). The hang persists — may be pre-existing (thread tests always blocked waiting for join). Native `Mutex.lock()` produces \"can't resolve symbol lock\" which is ALSO pre-existing (bare method name resolution issue unrelated to our session).\n**Note:** The param type change WAS reverted. Multi-format class name registration preserved.\n**Key files:** `compiler/src/compilation.rs` lines ~5582-5598.\n**Priority:** HIGH — blocks native e2e suite.\n\n\n**Symptom:** A pure-Haxe class containing a method with a parameter typed in the parameterised multi-arg arrow form `(name1:T1, name2:T2)-\u003eR` parses without error but the class is never registered with the TAST. Consumer code referencing the class then fails with `E0100: Cannot access field 'X': class not registered or field does not exist` on *every* member access (not just the function-typed param).\n\n**Repro (reproduced 2026-05-18 while building rayzor.concurrent.NumaPool):**\n```haxe\npackage rayzor.concurrent;\nclass NumaPool {\n    public function new() {}\n    public function parallelFor(items:Int, fn:(idx:Int, node:Int)-\u003eVoid):Void {\n        for (i in 0...items) fn(i, 0);\n    }\n    public static function global():NumaPool { return new NumaPool(); }\n}\n```\n`NumaPool.global()` from a consumer → `Cannot access field 'global'`. Switching the param type to the **curried** form `Int-\u003eInt-\u003eVoid` fixes it; the parser/TAST handles single-arg `Int-\u003eVoid` correctly too.\n\n**Workaround:** Use curried arrow syntax (`A-\u003eB-\u003eC`) for function-typed parameters in pure-Haxe classes until this is fixed. Drop the workaround in NumaPool.hx (and any other ML kernel class) once resolved.\n\n**Hypothesis:** TAST lowering of the parameterised arrow form silently fails partway through class field signature resolution and aborts class registration. Likely fix in `compiler/src/tast/ast_lowering.rs` around function-type parsing for class fields. Worth diagnostic: emit at least a parse error rather than silent invalidation.\n\n---\n\n\n`a.add(b).toString()` — returns Point2D but caller sees Ptr(Void), can't dispatch toString().\nFiles: `compiler/src/ir/hir_to_mir.rs`\n\n`new Point2D(3, 4)` stores zeros when class is from imported package.\nFiles: `compiler/src/compilation.rs`\n\nFloat `dt` parameter triggers move semantics. Extern GPU handles treated as owned.\nFiles: `ownership_analyzer.rs`, `hir_to_mir.rs`\n\n`TextureUsage.RENDER_ATTACHMENT` → \"class not registered\".\n\n\nJS handle-table implementation. Typed accessors all work (setInt32, getFloat, setDouble, etc.).\nByte-level `set`/`get` work in some builds but are fragile (import ordering sensitive).\n`StringTools.hex()` sometimes returns empty in WASM (separate issue).\n**Fixes applied:** handle table, unboxArg for DynamicValue args, qualified import creation for\nall-Unreachable stubs and unresolved CallDirect targets, Proxy auto-resolution with shortest-match.\n**Key files:** `src/main.rs` (JS handle table + Proxy), `compiler/src/codegen/wasm_backend.rs` (import phases).\n\nTwo-pass extern import: qualified names first, bare names redirect to qualified.\nProxy auto-resolves remaining bare imports with shortest-match preference.\nReduced from 18+ to ~12 bare imports. Remaining bare imports are for methods\nwithout qualified externs in module.extern_functions.\n**Key files:** `compiler/src/codegen/wasm_backend.rs` (two-pass extern, all-Unreachable stub imports, CallDirect target resolution).\n\nImplemented DynamicValue boxing (`_boxBool`/`_boxInt`) for Mutex imports in JS harness.\n\nComplex wrapper patterns produce type mismatches.\n\nMIR vector IR (vector_splat, vector_bin_op) not lowered to WASM SIMD128.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_known` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}